fix(console): assert the /dev/console write path stays sleepable

0145 routed cnopen/cnwrite to the kebab character device, so a userland
write to /dev/console now takes the same console_write() -> _cnputc()
path as kprintf.  That is deliberate (one ring, one USB tee, one panel),
but it means a userland write holds the console ring mutex and may
sleep, and the audit in docs/BRING-UP-STATE.md 76 asked whether that can
deadlock the machine and take the only observation channel with it.

It cannot, and the reasons are in the code this patch leaves alone:
kebab_usb_log() is a bounded non-blocking handoff that drops its bytes
when another CPU is inside it, and it drains synchronously only under
panic_active(); ud_enter()/ud_rx_enter() are IRQ-masking try-locks that
record a stranded owner and let ud_panic_takeover() reclaim it; _cnputc()
tees to the USB ring and then calls kebab_early_putc(), which takes no
lock at all.  The panic report never enters the console ring: it goes
paniclog_append_noflush -> consdebug_putc_unbuffered ->
console_write_unbuffered -> _cnputc, so a userland writer cannot hold a
lock the panic path needs.  cnread() returns EOF in kernel context with
no wait, and seltrue() reports EOF as readable, so a reader that ignores
EOF spins in userland only, where it holds no kernel lock and cannot
starve the USB event thread.

What the audit did not have was the invariant stated where a future
change would break it.  Refuse a console write that arrives with
interrupts masked: that is the state ud_enter() leaves behind for the
USB transmit gate (and of an interrupt handler), console_write() would
panic there with "Console I/O from interrupt-disabled context", and a
drain taken under the gate would park the kernel's own log behind the
USB port.  EDEADLK is diagnosable and unreachable from write(2), the
only caller today.

Also correct the 0145 claim that select() reports the device "writable
and never readable".  seltrue() reports both, and readable is the right
answer: read() returns 0 and EOF is a readable condition, exactly as
/dev/null behaves.  Reporting write-only would make select() wait on an
fd whose read() completes, so a job polling stdin would never see the
EOF.

Tests: -fsyntax-only with the real KEBAB flags for bsd/dev/kebab_ufs.c,
16 warnings before and after (13 pre-existing -Wdeclaration-after-
statement in that file); full DEVELOPMENT/ARM64/KEBAB build from a reset
tree with all 125 patches.  See docs/BRING-UP-STATE.md 76 for the
object-identity check that shows kprintf's mechanism did not change.
No hardware: the board is stranded.
--- a/bsd/dev/kebab_ufs.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/kebab_ufs.c
@@ -40,6 +40,7 @@
 #include <vm/vm_kern.h>
 #include <pexpert/pexpert.h>
 #include <pexpert/device_tree.h>
+#include <arm/machine_routines.h>
 #include <libkern/libkern.h>
 
 #include "kebab_ufs.h"
@@ -822,7 +823,8 @@
  * a userland write reaches the USB console and the screen from one place.
  *
  * Reads return EOF: there is no keyboard or serial input behind this node.
- * Select reports the device writable and never readable.
+ * Select reports the device ready in both directions (seltrue), so a reader is
+ * handed the EOF instead of blocking on a port that can never produce a byte.
  */
 bool
 kebab_console_available(void)
@@ -856,6 +858,21 @@
 {
 	char buf[64];
 
+	/*
+	 * Audit guard (docs/BRING-UP-STATE.md 76): this is where userland bytes
+	 * enter the kernel console, and console_write() takes the console ring
+	 * mutex, so the caller has to be a sleepable thread.  Interrupts masked is
+	 * exactly the state ud_enter() leaves behind for the USB transmit gate, and
+	 * the state of an interrupt handler; console_write() would panic there
+	 * ("Console I/O from interrupt-disabled context") and a drain under the gate
+	 * would park the log behind the USB port.  The only caller today is write(2);
+	 * if that ever changes, fail the write with a diagnosable error instead of
+	 * losing the kernel's own console.
+	 */
+	if (!ml_get_interrupts_enabled()) {
+		return EDEADLK;
+	}
+
 	while (uio_resid(uio) > 0) {
 		size_t want = MIN(sizeof(buf), (size_t)uio_resid(uio));
 		int error = uiomove(buf, (int)want, uio);
@@ -878,6 +895,15 @@
 kebab_console_select(dev_t dev, int flag, __unused void *wql,
     __unused struct proc *p)
 {
+	/*
+	 * seltrue() reports readable and writable.  Readable is the truthful
+	 * answer, not an oversight: read() returns 0 immediately and EOF is a
+	 * readable condition, so this node behaves like /dev/null.  Reporting
+	 * write-only would make select() wait on an fd whose read() completes, and a
+	 * job polling stdin for input would never see the EOF.  A program that
+	 * ignores EOF spins in userland but holds no kernel lock, so it cannot
+	 * starve the USB event thread (BRING-UP-STATE 76).
+	 */
 	return seltrue(dev, flag, p);
 }
 
